            "rubricHtml": "<h3>Questions 31–40</h3><p>Complete the notes below.</p><p>Write <strong>ONE WORD ONLY</strong> for each answer.</p>",
            "bodyHtml": "<div class=\"summary\"><div class=\"notes-title\">After Action Review Process</div><div class=\"sub-head\">Background</div><ul class=\"notes-list\"> <li>A way of evaluating during a project in order to make improvements</li> <li>Developed by the {{31}}.</li> </ul><div class=\"sub-head\">Benefits</div><ul class=\"notes-list\"> <li>Not just for large projects &mdash; can also be used after: <ul class=\"notes-list\"> <li>a staff meeting</li> <li>a {{32}} incident</li> </ul> </li> <li>Develops better team communication because of the emphasis on {{33}}.</li> </ul><div class=\"sub-head\">What is an After Action Review?</div><ul class=\"notes-list\"> <li>It&rsquo;s a meeting that: <ul class=\"notes-list\"> <li>concentrates on results and the {{34}} for them</li> <li>encourages participation</li> <li>stresses the need to {{35}} each other</li> </ul> </li> </ul><div class=\"sub-head\">Things to remember when conducting an After Action Review</div><ul class=\"notes-list\"> <li>Use an external facilitator (so stronger team members cannot dominate)</li> <li>To encourage participation, get the group&rsquo;s thoughts in {{36}}.</li> <li>Remember to use {{37}} questions</li> <li>Give any recommendations to other team {{38}}.</li> <li>Make sure you follow up on ideas and provide {{39}} for employees</li> <li>Remember to check the {{40}} often</li> </ul></div>"
          }
        ]
